// The landing page is read by people with no account and no context, so it is
// a single readable column of prose and links rather than the dense app chrome
// the rest of the site uses. It shares its page language — measure, paper, type
// scale, buttons — with the Genesis League pitch page it links to and with the
// questionnaire beyond that, because they are read one after another and a
// change of measure between them reads as a change of site. That language lives
// in app/styles/prose-form.styl.
//
// NO BOXES AND NO ICONS. The page is a directory, and a directory drawn as a
// grid of cards becomes a marketing page the moment there are three of them.
// Hierarchy is carried by spacing and hairline rules, which is what lets a
// twelve-entry list stay scannable without any of it shouting.
//
// THE PAGE IS A FIELD, and most of the marks that say so were already on it.
// What the existing rules become, none of them changing weight:
//
//   the two 2px ink rules   the goal lines
//   the hero above the first, and the space below the last   the end zones
//   the 1px ink band rules  the ten-yard lines
//   the hairlines between entries   the yard lines between them
//
// NO BORDER AROUND THE PAGE. Sidelines and end lines drawn as a hairline
// rectangle were tried and removed. Whatever it is called, a rectangle around a
// directory of four groups is the grid of cards the paragraph above forbids,
// drawn once instead of four times — and it read as a container the moment it
// was on screen, which is the failure that paragraph is describing.
//
// SO THE FIELD IS MARKED FROM INSIDE, by the yard numbers. They are what makes
// a set of parallel rules read as a field rather than as a list — the mark a
// reader identifies before he has thought about it, and the reason a field is
// recognisable from the top row with no sideline in view. Being painted ON the
// ground, they cost nothing at the page's edges, which is where the border went
// wrong.
//
// NOT TICKS ON THE BAR. A row of ticks along each band rule was tried in an
// earlier pass and the operator removed them, and this is deliberately not that
// gesture returning in another costume: a tick decorates the rule it sits on,
// and a yard number is a mark on the ground that a rule happens to run past.
//
// THEY COUNT DOWN toward the goal line the page ends on, so reading the
// directory top to bottom is a drive. The direction is also what makes the
// sequence survive an edit — a fifth section becomes the 10 and the drive still
// lands, where counting up would run off the field.
// The hash row hangs in the page's own right gutter, so its offset is that
// gutter's width negated — prose_page() sets 32px, and 22px on a phone. Stated
// as its own variable because `-$var` does not negate inside a property, and
// kept beside the numbers it is derived from so the two cannot drift.
$sideline_offset = -32px
$sideline_offset_phone = -22px

.landing-surface
  prose_surface()

.landing
  prose_page_wide()

  h2
    font-family $prose_mono
    font-weight $prose_bold
    color $prose_ink
    line-height 1.25
    letter-spacing -0.01em

  p
    margin 0 0 1em
    font-family inherit
    line-height inherit
    letter-spacing normal

// Qualified with the element, like the lede and the deck below: the `.landing
// p` reset above is (0,1,1) and silently wins over a bare class.
.landing p.landing__eyebrow
  prose_eyebrow()

// No rule of its own. The first section's band rule is the masthead rule — see
// prose_section_band().
//
// THE MARK HANGS IN THE MARGIN, and the text does not move for it. Putting the
// mark in a column of its own indents the headline past the page's own left
// edge, which breaks the one alignment the whole page is built on — the lede,
// every section label and every entry label share that edge, and the header is
// the worst place to lose it.
//
// So it is taken OUT OF FLOW into the space left of the measure. The text block
// keeps the full width it had before the mark existed, flush left.
//
// The mark comes AFTER the text in the DOM, so the copy is what a screen reader
// reaches first; which side it paints on is a layout question.
.landing__hero
  position relative
  padding-bottom 0

  // THE BREAKPOINT IS SET BY THE NAV DRAWER, NOT BY THE PAGE GUTTER, and that
  // is the whole reason it is this high. The arithmetic that suggests itself —
  // measure 880px, so the gutter is (viewport - 880) / 2, and the mark needs
  // 104px plus a 32px gap, therefore 1160px — is answering the wrong question:
  // the mark is not hanging into empty space, it is hanging into the column the
  // app's drawer already occupies. Measured, the mark's left edge clears the
  // drawer's right edge by 56px at 1440 and OVERLAPS it by 24px at 1280, so a
  // 1160px rule paints the mark on top of the menu. 1400px is the narrowest
  // width that still clears it.
  //
  // Anything narrower puts the mark back in the flow above the header, which is
  // what the phone layout does and for the same reason: there is no room beside
  // the text, and the text keeping its own left edge matters more than the mark
  // keeping its position.
  @media (min-width 1400px)
    .route-diagram
      position absolute
      // `top 0` and not `top 6px`: the base rule already carries margin-top 6px,
      // and an absolutely positioned box takes BOTH, which silently doubles the
      // optical nudge that aligns the mark with the eyebrow.
      top 0
      right 100%
      margin-right 32px

  @media (max-width 1399px)
    display flex
    flex-direction column-reverse
    align-items flex-start
    gap 28px

.landing__hero-text
  min-width 0

// `display block` is load-bearing — an inline svg sits on the text baseline and
// carries a few pixels of descender gap beneath it.
//
// Sized by WIDTH alone, with the viewBox holding the aspect ratio, so the mark
// cannot be stretched out of the proportions it was matched to.
.route-diagram
  display block
  flex-shrink 0
  width 104px
  height auto
  margin-top 6px

  @media (max-width 640px)
    width 68px
    margin-top 0

// A route is a stroked path and must not be filled, or the area between its
// legs floods; a head is nothing but fill. One ink, two properties.
.route-diagram__stroke
  fill none
  stroke $prose_rule_heavy
  stroke-width 15

.route-diagram__fill
  fill $prose_rule_heavy
  stroke none

// The opening sentence is the page's headline, not its first paragraph: set at
// display size in the mono that is this site's signature, at a measure short
// enough to be taken in rather than read. Mono sets loose and even, so at this
// size it needs negative tracking and tight leading — without them it reads as
// a terminal dump, which is the failure mode of every mono headline.
.landing h1.landing__lede
  margin 0
  max-width 26ch
  // Balanced, so the headline never leaves one word alone on its second line.
  // `text-wrap` degrades to `normal` where it is unsupported, so this is polish
  // that cannot break a layout.
  text-wrap balance
  font-family $prose_mono
  font-weight $prose_bold
  font-size clamp(25px, 3.1vw, 34px)
  line-height 1.3
  letter-spacing -0.025em
  color $prose_ink

  @media (max-width 640px)
    max-width none

// The qualifying sentence, in the body face so it reads as the explanation of
// the headline rather than as a second headline.
.landing p.landing__deck
  margin 26px 0 0
  max-width 46ch
  font-size 1.125em
  line-height 1.55
  letter-spacing normal
  color $prose_body
  text-wrap pretty

  @media (max-width 640px)
    margin-top 20px
    font-size 1.0625em

// The two promoted destinations, set as links rather than buttons. Mono at
// label size so they read as the same kind of object as the directory entries
// below — which is what they are, lifted to the top.
.landing p.landing__hero-links
  display flex
  flex-wrap wrap
  gap 28px
  margin 32px 0 0
  font-family $prose_mono
  font-weight $prose_bold
  font-size 0.9375em
  letter-spacing -0.01em

// A rule opens each section and the spacing inside it carries the rest:
// entries within a section are closer than the sections are to each other, and
// the two lines of an entry closer still.
//
// `position relative` is for the yard number, which is taken out of flow so
// that a mark at display size cannot push the head row around.
//
// THE PADDING IS THE NUMBER'S BAND, and it is on the SECTION rather than on the
// head row because a rotated numeral is taller than the head — reserved on the
// head alone, it hung down into the first entry and sat on the last word of a
// description. Reserved here, every row in the section stops short of it.
//
// The band does not shorten the rules. A border spans the padding box, so the
// ten-yard line and the hairlines below it still run the full measure and pass
// behind the number — which is what a yard line does.
.landing__section
  prose_section_band()
  position relative
  margin-top 32px
  padding-right 56px

  &:first-of-type
    prose_section_band(2px)

  // THE HASH MARKS. A field's yard lines are crossed by a row of short marks
  // at every yard between them, and that texture is most of what a field looks
  // like up close — the long lines say where you are and the hashes say the
  // ground is measured continuously.
  //
  // TWO LENGTHS, ONE STRIP. The short mark is every yard; the long one falls
  // where a five-yard line would and is drawn further out, so the row reads in
  // fives rather than as an undifferentiated comb. Two repeating gradients on
  // one pseudo-element, each sized to its own tick length.
  //
  // ALONG THE RIGHT EDGE, WHICH IS THE SIDELINE THE NUMBERS FACE. A field
  // number is painted to be read from the sideline nearest it, and the hash row
  // belongs on that same side — the number and the ticks are one edge of the
  // field seen together, not two marks on opposite margins. Read down the page
  // the row now goes prose, then the turned numeral, then the hashes outboard
  // of it, which is the order a sideline puts them in.
  //
  // IN THE PAGE'S OWN GUTTER, out past the right end of the rules and past the
  // number's band. The left edge INSIDE the measure is the most spoken-for
  // column on the page — the lede, every section head and every entry label
  // stand on it — and the right edge inside the measure is the number's. Hung
  // in the padding the row touches neither, and the marks sit just outside the
  // lines they measure, which is where a sideline is.
  //
  // OUTBOARD OF THE NUMBER, NOT SHARING ITS BAND. The two were in one corridor
  // once and the band had to be 72px to hold both. The number keeps the band at
  // its own width and the ticks hang past it in the gutter, so the prose column
  // pays nothing for having them on the same side.
  //
  // The long tick is anchored to the strip's INNER edge and grows outward, the
  // mirror of what it did on the left — background-position is `left top` here
  // so the five-yard mark reaches away from the measure rather than into it.
  //
  // NOT THE TICKS THAT WERE REMOVED FROM THE BAR. Those were a row ALONG a band
  // rule, decorating the rule. These run down the field between the rules and
  // cross none of them.
  &::before
    content ''
    position absolute
    top 0
    bottom 0
    right $sideline_offset
    width 12px
    background-image repeating-linear-gradient(to bottom, $prose_rule 0 1px, transparent 1px 90px), repeating-linear-gradient(to bottom, $prose_rule 0 1px, transparent 1px 18px)
    background-size 12px 100%, 7px 100%
    background-repeat no-repeat, no-repeat
    background-position left top, left top

  // The right band is now the numeral and its clearance and nothing else: 48px
  // of turned numeral plus the 8px before the prose. It was 72px when the hash
  // row shared it.
  @media (max-width 640px)
    margin-top 26px
    padding-right 38px

    &::before
      right $sideline_offset_phone

// THE YARD NUMBER. Large, and painted the colour of a rule rather than of text
// — it is a mark on the ground, not something to read, and at ink weight it
// would be the loudest thing on the page and would read as a heading with a
// number in it.
//
// AT THE RIGHT EDGE OF THE MEASURE, in the band the section reserves for it,
// starting level with the ten-yard line it belongs to. Field numbers sit inside
// a sideline, and the page's left edge is spoken for — the lede, every section
// head and every entry label stand on it. The right edge is the free one.
//
// TURNED, BECAUSE A FIELD NUMBER IS TURNED. It is painted to be read from the
// sideline it sits nearest, not from the end zone, and that is most of why a
// number on grass reads as a field rather than as a label. `writing-mode` is
// what turns it: an upright numeral in the same place is a figure beside a
// heading, and the same numeral rotated is paint on the ground.
//
// It is also what makes the mark affordable. Turned, it occupies a 40px-wide
// strip and any height it likes; upright at a size worth seeing it would be a
// 90px-wide block, and there is no 90px of measure to give up.
//
// IN THE PAINT FACE, which is the one thing on this site that is neither of the
// two faces. Mono was never a candidate — its even widths read as a monospace
// setting of a number — and Inter 700, which this was, reads as a user
// interface: it is a screen face at a weight meant for labels. A field numeral
// is heavy at roughly normal width, six feet by four, and Chivo 900 is the
// nearest thing to that. app/index.html carries the comparison it won.
//
// $prose_rule, which is ONE PAINT FOR EVERY FIELD MARKING — this number, the
// hash marks outboard of it, and the lattice in the end zone. It is the
// faintest grey the palette has, and paint is the one thing on the page that
// can afford it: a mark this size is legible at a contrast a line of prose
// would disappear at, and everything painted here is decorative by
// construction, carrying nothing a reader has to be able to make out.
//
// It was $prose_rule_strong, one step darker, which is the value for a LINE a
// reader is meant to notice — and at 48px that read as ink, so the number
// competed with the head across the row from it instead of sitting under it.
.landing__yard
  position absolute
  // 14px against prose_section_band()'s own 16px of padding-top: a turned
  // numeral has its side bearing above it rather than a cap height, so matching
  // the numbers leaves it visibly low against the head beside it.
  top 14px
  right 0
  writing-mode vertical-rl
  font-family $prose_paint
  font-weight 900
  font-size 48px
  line-height 1
  letter-spacing 0.02em
  color $prose_rule
  user-select none

  @media (max-width 640px)
    top 12px
    font-size 30px

// A standing label under the band rule, not a headline. The entries below are
// set larger than it and that is the newspaper order — a section head names
// where you are and the items are what you came to read. What separates it from
// the muted labels elsewhere on the site is the ink and the rule above it,
// which is enough and does not cost a fourth size in the type scale.
//
// ON THE LEFT EDGE, WITH EVERYTHING ELSE. It was right-aligned in the rail's
// label column, because on the left edge it read as one more item in the list
// and face and size were losing that argument alone. The sideline settles it:
// the head is the first thing inside a segment of the field, under that
// segment's own ten-yard line, and it no longer needs to leave the page's one
// alignment to say so. What it costs to leave is the whole point — the lede,
// every entry label and this head now share one edge at every width, and the
// right-aligned version had a hundred and thirty pixels of dead gutter to the
// left of every section head.
//
// It also drops the phone exception, which existed only because the rail
// collapses there and a right-aligned head would have gone to the far edge of
// the screen. There is one alignment now and no width where it changes.
//
// QUALIFIED WITH THE ELEMENT, like the eyebrow and the lede. The `.landing h2`
// block above is (0,1,1) and silently beats a bare class — and it beats it on
// exactly the four properties this label is trying to change (family, weight,
// colour, tracking) while leaving font-size to come through, so an unqualified
// rule here renders at the new SIZE in the old FACE and reads as a change that
// landed.
// A STEP OFF INK, and the entry labels are why. Both were $prose_ink, so the
// head and the twelve destinations under it were the same colour, and a head
// competing with the things it names is the argument the right-aligned version
// was trying to win with position. $prose_body puts the head in the layer it
// belongs to — the wayfinding layer, with the blurb beside it and the yard
// number at the end of the row — and leaves ink to the entry labels, which are
// the only things on the page a reader came to click.
//
// Not $prose_muted: that is the blurb's colour, and a head set in it is a
// caption. One step, not two.
.landing h2.landing__section-title
  prose_section_label()
  color $prose_body

// One line under the heading saying what the group is for, so a reader can
// skip a whole section without reading its entries. Muted, because it is
// orientation rather than content. It sits in the rail's prose column so the
// section head and the entry labels below share one left edge.
//
// A STEP SMALLER THAN THE DESCRIPTIONS IT INTRODUCES. At the page's body size
// it was the largest text in its row and the third largest on the page, so the
// band rule said a section was beginning and the type said the blurb was the
// thing to read — colour was carrying that distinction alone against a size
// that argued the other way. Dropping one step puts the row's weight back on
// the head and the entries, which is the order the rule already sets.
//
// Balanced rather than pretty: these are one or two lines, and pretty only
// guards the LAST line, which on a two-line blurb leaves a full first line
// above four words. Balance splits them evenly.
.landing p.landing__section-blurb
  margin 0
  font-size 0.875em
  line-height 1.5
  text-wrap balance
  color $prose_muted

.landing__section-head
  prose_rail()
  margin-bottom 18px

  @media (max-width 640px)
    margin-bottom 16px

    .landing__section-title
      margin-bottom 0.6em

.landing__links
  margin 0
  padding 0
  list-style none

// A hairline above each entry separates them without drawing a box around
// anything, which keeps the page reading as one column of paper. The first
// entry takes none, so the rule never doubles up under the band above it.
.landing__link
  prose_rail()
  padding-top 11px
  margin-top 11px
  border-top 1px solid $prose_rule

  &:first-child
    padding-top 0
    margin-top 0
    border-top none

// The destination, in the rail. Set in the mono so the twelve of them form a
// column the eye can run down without reading a word of the prose beside them.
// The note wraps under the label rather than beside it, since the rail is
// sized for one and not for both.
.landing p.landing__link-label
  margin 0
  display flex
  flex-wrap wrap
  align-items baseline
  gap 4px 10px
  font-family $prose_mono
  font-weight $prose_bold
  font-size 0.9375em
  line-height 1.45
  letter-spacing -0.01em

  @media (max-width 640px)
    margin-bottom 0.35em

// An entry with no destination yet. FULL INK, like every linked sibling — the
// muted grey this carried read as the entry being faded out, which is the
// register of something disabled rather than of something still being built.
// What marks it as not clickable is the missing underline, which is the same
// signal every other page uses and does not cost the label its weight in the
// column. Its `note` says why there is no link.
//
// Set explicitly rather than left to inherit: prose_page() colours the page
// $prose_body and only an anchor gets $prose_ink, so dropping this rule
// entirely would leave the label a step lighter than its linked siblings —
// a third state, and a quieter version of the fade being removed here.
.landing__link-label--inert
  color $prose_ink

// The one thing on the page that qualifies rather than names. Tiny, mono,
// muted — it has to be legible beside the label without competing with it,
// since its whole job is to stop a reader hunting for a link that is not there.
.landing__link-note
  font-family $prose_mono
  font-weight $prose_bold
  font-size 0.6875em
  letter-spacing 0.06em
  text-transform uppercase
  color $prose_muted

// No max-width: the rail already holds the measure, and a second limit inside
// it would only ever be the binding one at some width nobody chose.
//
// A STEP UNDER THE PAGE'S BODY SIZE, which is the one size change the field
// asked for. 17px is a READING size, set for the paragraph it was designed
// against — and none of these is a paragraph. They are one-line captions in a
// list of fourteen, and at reading size half of them took two lines each, so
// the directory ran a screen and a half and the lines that structure it were
// too far apart to read as a field at all. At 16px most of them fit one line,
// which halves the page and doubles the rules on it.
.landing p.landing__link-description
  margin 0
  font-size 0.9412em
  line-height 1.55
  color $prose_body
  text-wrap pretty

// THE SECOND GOAL LINE. It takes the MASTHEAD's 2px rather than a section
// band's 1px, so the page is bracketed by the same weight top and bottom —
// that is what says the directory ended rather than ran out, and it is what
// makes the space between it and the frame below an end zone rather than
// trailing whitespace. It carries nothing, which is why it is a bare div.
// THE END ZONE, and it is a bounded, painted band rather than the space that
// happened to be left under the last rule.
//
// TWO BOUNDARIES, BOTH AT THE GOAL LINE'S WEIGHT. The rule above is the goal
// line the directory closes on; the rule below is the end line, and an end zone
// with only one of them is an edge with space under it. This is not the border
// that was tried and removed — that one enclosed the whole page and read as a
// container. This encloses the six inches of field that a boundary is FOR, and
// the thing it bounds is the only part of the page with no content in it.
//
// THE SIDELINES ARE WHERE THE PAINT STOPS. The lattice runs to the measure's
// edges and end, so the zone is closed left and right without two more rules.
//
// A DIAMOND LATTICE, WHICH IS THE OLD DESIGN. Before end zones were solid
// colour with a wordmark dropped in, they were ruled off in diamonds — a
// diagonal lattice painted the width of the zone — and that is a pattern this
// palette can actually draw, because it is made of the page's own hairline
// repeated rather than of a second surface colour.
//
// TWO GRADIENTS AT OPPOSED 45s, not one. A single family of stripes is a hatch
// and reads as shading; the diamonds only appear where the second family
// crosses the first, so both are load-bearing and neither is decoration on the
// other.
//
// A 26px PITCH, NEARLY TWICE THE HATCH IT REPLACES, and the pitch is what
// decides whether this reads as diamonds at all. Two families lay down twice
// the ink of one, so the hatch's own 14px darkened the zone into a grey panel —
// which is the solid-colour end zone this pattern exists instead of. 18px was
// tried against the rendered page and still read as mesh: the cells were small
// enough that the eye took the texture and not the shape. At 26px the diamonds
// are individually legible and the zone is lighter than the hatch was.
.landing__end
  margin-top 32px
  border-top 2px solid $prose_rule_heavy
  border-bottom 2px solid $prose_rule_heavy
  background-image repeating-linear-gradient(45deg, $prose_rule 0 1px, transparent 1px 26px), repeating-linear-gradient(-45deg, $prose_rule 0 1px, transparent 1px 26px)

  @media (max-width 640px)
    margin-top 26px

// THE PAINT IN THE END ZONE. Sized to fill the width rather than to a point on
// the type scale, which is what end zone lettering does — it is set to the zone
// it is painted in. The tracking is doing that fitting: at 0.22em the wordmark
// spans the measure at the size below, and the two move together.
//
// text-indent cancels the trailing letter-space that tracking leaves after the
// last L, which otherwise sits the whole line a few pixels left of centre — the
// same correction the floating action makes with asymmetric padding.
//
// It clamps rather than scaling by viewport, so the phone gets a wordmark that
// still fills its zone without a 52px line breaking in two.
.landing__end-mark
  display block
  padding 44px 0
  text-align center
  text-indent 0.22em
  font-family $prose_paint
  font-weight 900
  font-size clamp(24px, 5.9vw, 52px)
  line-height 1
  letter-spacing 0.22em
  text-transform uppercase
  // A STEP DARKER THAN THE LATTICE IT SITS ON, which is the one place the
  // one-paint rule bends and has to. Everything else painted on this page sits
  // on the bare ground; this sits on ruling in the same grey, and at the same
  // value the letters and the lattice dissolve into each other.
  color $prose_rule_strong
  user-select none

  @media (max-width 640px)
    padding 30px 0
